WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
AI-assisted documentation is only as reliable as the source material it is grounded in.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:26.600
Useful inputs can include architecture diagrams, README files, API specifications, infrastructure-as-code, deployment manifests, cloud inventory exports, CMDB records, repository structure, network diagrams, ticket history, approved scan summaries, logs, and telemetry summaries.

3
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.700
The more authoritative the evidence, the more useful the documentation can be.

4
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.460
Different sources answer different questions.

5
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.400
API specifications reveal interfaces and methods.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.740
Infrastructure-as-code can show cloud resources, network rules, identities, and dependencies.

7
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.400
Repository structure can reveal services, packages, and deployment patterns.

8
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.380
Ticket history can explain why a service exists or when an integration changed.

9
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.280
Cloud exports can show what is actually deployed right now.

10
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.800
Source quality should be documented.

11
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.740
A current deployment manifest is usually stronger evidence than an old diagram.

12
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.640
A system owner confirmation may resolve ambiguity that code alone cannot.

13
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:14.940
A scan summary from an authorized tool can support exposure analysis, but it should not be treated as the only source of truth.

14
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.840
Each source has age, scope, and reliability limits.

15
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.000
AI should be instructed to work from provided evidence.

16
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.460
If a fact is not present, the output should identify the gap instead of inventing details.

17
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:40.640
This is especially important for security-relevant claims such as authentication behavior, encryption, data sensitivity, public exposure, ownership, logging, or third-party access.

18
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.040
Good source gathering also protects confidentiality.

19
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:51.820
Use approved data handling practices, avoid feeding sensitive secrets into tools, and keep the workflow inside authorized boundaries.

20
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:59.680
The documentation process should improve security visibility without creating a new exposure of private architecture or credentials.
